---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
---
<Layout title="About">
  <Header />
  <section id = "about">
    <h1>About Me</h1>

<img style = "width: 200px;
              height: 200px;
              object-fit: cover;
              object-position: 50% 20%;
              float: right;
              padding: 1rem;
              border-radius: 50%" src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/00_about_me/about-me.png`}/>
<p>
Hi, I'm <a href="https://github.com/joshie-wu">Josh</a> and welcome to my blog!
</p>

<p>
I am interested in all things data, but I mostly write about education and politics. However, I occasionally dabble in more meta topics, such as which graph to use and my perspectives on certain statistical models. 
</p>

<p>
On the technical side, my go-to tools are <b>R</b> and <b>Python</b>. Because of <a href="https://tidyverse.org/">tidyverse</a>, R is my absolute favorite for data cleaning, pre-processing, and visualization. For more complicated workflows such as machine learning, I prefer to use Python. 
</p>

<p> 
The world is filled with data, and most of it is freely accessible and open source! However, while people are becoming more and more data literate, there are still some misunderstandings. I started <b> I Quant Data</b> as a way to make data more accessible by creating easily interpretable visualizations and showing people what can be done with publicly available data sets. 
</p>

<p> 
To be clear, although I cover topics on education and politics, this blog is not intended to give advice on how to run public services, but merely to give people more insight into their day-to-day lives. I also want to make data less intimidating and show people how fun it can be to play with the data in their daily lives.. Above all, I created this blog as an outlet to write.
</p>

<p>
I have an M.A. in Statistics as well as a B.A. in Mathematics from Columbia University, where I developed a love for applied statistics. 
</p>


  </section>
</Layout>